  -> paired sd (121) is below either arm's (141); quote the PAIRED figure.

  Prediction on record, made before any measurement: -2242 pcm,
  because that is what the environment-variable route (which zeroes the whole
  run's transport temperature, S(alpha,beta) moderators included) measured on
  this same case in gh:#193's pricing table. The question is whether the new
  PER-NUCLIDE hook reaches the same set of nuclides that did.

  VERDICT:
    FAILED as stated: -153 ± 43 pcm is +2089 pcm from the recorded
    -2242 pcm (48.8 sigma). Per the hand-off this is a REAL FINDING, not a
    failure. But it is NOT the hand-off's stated explanation -- that the per-nuclide
    hook reaches a different set of nuclides than zeroing the temperature did, the
    S(alpha,beta) moderators in particular. Reading the code refutes that mechanism:
      - XsSource::Pointwise never passes temp_k to recon.eval_mt, and every nuclide
        on this case is HIGH tier, so zeroing the temperature moves no cross section;
      - Nuclide::sample_thermal takes NO temperature argument at all -- the S(alpha,beta)
        tables are fixed at construction, so the env var cannot reach the bound-atom law;
      - free_gas_kt(temp) is the ONLY consumer of the run temperature in the collision
        kernel (until GitHub #313, 2026-09-30, made it the data temperature), and with_target_at_rest makes that same function return 0.0.
    The two routes are therefore mechanically identical on this case, and the gap is
    CRATE DRIFT: the reference predates gh:#193's F-19 inelastic fix and op-tm9f /
    op-og56. Check the unablated arm against the recorded k_eff 1.40546 and against
    OpenMC's p 0.4842 / eps 1.5043 before reading the difference as a hook defect.
    Note also that the -2242 pcm reference carries a ONE-SEED within-run
    error bar (±323 pcm), not an ensemble one; part of any gap may be that.
EXIT 0
